# 孫子 (Sun Tzu) — learning from the flaw inside a success

## Answer

Half the principle is sound and half needs a limit. The learnable signal is a *contrast* between two attempts of the same task that differ in one step: a success next to a total failure, or a better success next to a worse one. A total failure that no attempt has ever passed, with no relabel and no probe, is the weakest teacher — but it is not nothing, and the harness must not stop logging it. The sharper point for R12.7 is that a passing task is not the opposite of a failure: the mistakes inside a success (the workaround, the retry, the untested step) are invisible in a pass and must be recorded as such, and the next child should recombine trusted steps from more than one recorded success rather than copy one parent. So: keep R12.2–R12.3 as they are and add the contrast and the passenger mark; change R12.7 to record the path in parts. This is conditional on each step having its own check (see What I need to know).

Hmm, that's more than 2 sentences. The brief says one or two sentences. Let me compress to two.

## How I decided

1. I first state the principle as a comparison, because a comparison is the only form in which it can be checked. My reading of the brief; the researcher's findings F4 (all-correct and all-wrong groups give zero advantage) and F8 (goals of intermediate difficulty) are the same statement in RL. The harness already has the comparison: parent vs child on a named measure (the brief's R12.7).

2. Where it holds — no reckoning, no read. My #1: 「夫未戰而廟算勝者，得算多也…多算勝，少算不勝，而況於無算乎？」 `quoted` — "more 算 wins, fewer does not, how much less none at all." A total failure whose check has never passed and which carries no measure is the 無算 case: there is no second reckoning to compare against, so no difference to read. The researcher reaches the same place from GA (F1–F4).

3. Where it holds — the harm inside the benefit is already a rule of mine. My #6: 「是故智者之慮，必雜於利害…故不盡知用兵之害者，則不能盡知用兵之利也。」 `quoted` — the wise deliberate with harm and benefit mixed; whoever does not know the harm fully does not know the benefit fully. Applied: a success path that records only the steps, and not the step that almost failed or the workaround that carried it, is the benefit without the harm, and the next run cannot know what it is buying.

4. Where it holds — my oldest precedent is a judgement made from the imperfections inside successes, not from success or failure. My #20 `attributed`: all six houses had divided Jin; Sunzi said Fan and Zhonghang would fall first, from the small land unit, the heavy tax and the many officials ("the land area was small and the officials in office were many… the ruler became arrogant and his ministers wasteful"), and that Zhao would hold from the largest unit and no new taxes. Same success at the surface, different defects inside. The label is `attributed` — a narrated dialogue in the bamboo text, not the main thirteen chapters.

5. Where it holds — the harness's borrow-parent/change-one-thing loop is my #12 in the simplest form. My #12: 「凡戰者，以正合，以奇勝。…戰勢不過奇正，奇正之變，不可勝窮也。」 `quoted` — engage with the regular, win with the irregular. The parent path is the regular; the child's change is the irregular. The researcher's inference 1 (given) reads this loop as a (1+1)-ES from F17; that is their reading, not mine, and I take it as a description, not a source of authority.

6. Where it breaks — a total failure is the harness's own probe, and my rules say probes are how the other side is learned. My #17: 「故策之而知得失之計，作之而知動靜之理，形之而知死生之地，角之而知有餘不足之處。」 `quoted` — reckon, stir, shape, test in contact. A failing test is a 角 (contact test); the pass/fail of a check is itself a probe. So "we learn nothing from total failure" is false as a general rule: we learn *where the boundary is* from a failure we can place against a boundary we have. 

7. Where it breaks — my #15 places the cause of every defeat in a changeable source, not in heaven. My #15: 「凡此六者，非天之災，將之過也。」 `quoted` — "these six are not calamities from heaven but the general's faults." R12.3's six whys already do this for a total failure. Reading the principle as "total failures are worthless" would throw away the very case #15 names.

8. Where it breaks — a success may belong to the enemy, not to you. My #2: 「不可勝在己，可勝在敵…故曰：勝可知，而不可為。」 `quoted` — being unbeatable is in your own hands, being beatable is in the enemy's; victory can be known but cannot be made. A task that passed because the engine was swapped (the brief's n=3 case: a fake engine returned nothing and the agent called the real binary) is a success the *conditions* gave, not the design. If R12.7 records only `ok`, it rewards the defect. My #4 (「知彼知己…」 `quoted`) is the rule to keep self and other apart.

9. Where it breaks — the unit. R12.7 records a path for the whole task. GA's signal is on parts: the building-block hypothesis says adaptation works on "low order, low defining-length schemata with above average fitness" and builds "better and better strings from the best partial solutions of past samplings" (Goldberg 1989 p.41, as quoted by Wikipedia; `reported`). A whole-path unit cannot tell a trusted step from a passenger step, and copying the whole path copies both — the genetic-hitchhiking effect (a linked "neutral or even slightly deleterious" allele rises with the beneficial sweep; `reported`). This is the gap I would fix in R12.7.

10. The size of "change at least one thing" should follow the win rate, not a fixed rule. My #10: 「故兵無成勢，無恒形」 `quoted` — no fixed 勢, no constant form. The researcher's F19–F21 give the GA origin: success-based step-size control, "usually chosen as 1/5," and F21 says it is "mostly superseded" as a control. So the 1/5 is a direction, reported, never a gate; the harness should record how often children beat parents and let that steer the size.

11. Keep more than one parent. My #18: 「圍師必闕，窮寇勿迫」 `quoted` — leave a gap for a surrounded army. A single best path is a surrounded army; the researcher's F28–F35 (deception, novelty, MAP-Elites, lexicase) are the same warning. My #13: 「君命有所不受」 `quoted` — a ruler's command is not always accepted: a parent path is a command the child may refuse in part.

12. What I would not change: R12.2 and R12.3 already cover the failure side correctly — the failure is logged even when a retry passes, and the insight goes six whys to a changeable source. The change is to add the contrast and to put the imperfections of successes into R12.7.

## Proposed rule text

Then three/four blocks.

A. R12.2 — add after "...the path to the evidence (the data) in the run folder.":
Text:
"Where the same check has also passed on another run of the same category, the record names the step at which this attempt differed from the nearest passing attempt, or states that no step differed; where no passing attempt exists, the record says so."
Check (can fail): A reviewer compares a planted set of two records of the same check — one passed, one failed — where the failed record names no differing step. The check fails if that record is accepted; it passes when the record names the step or says no step differed. (Planted wrong input: failed record without the field.)

B. R12.3 — add:
"For a failure with no passing sibling, no relabel and no probe, the insight states in writing that the check has never passed and names no root cause it cannot contrast; for a failure with a passing sibling, the named root cause is the differing step recorded under R12.2, or the insight says why that step is not the cause."
