## What my own research added, with sources

- Building blocks, not the best individual. Goldberg, as quoted by [Genetic algorithm — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genetic_algorithm) (raw wikitext, "The building block hypothesis"): "Short, low order, and highly fit schemata are sampled, recombined, and resampled to form strings of potentially higher fitness… we construct better and better strings from the best partial solutions of past samplings." Label: the quotation is `verified` as printed on that page; Goldberg 1989 was not opened. The same page carries the caveat, `verified` as printed: "Despite the lack of consensus regarding the validity of the building-block hypothesis, it has been consistently evaluated and used as reference." What it adds: the harness's unit of learning should be the step/schema, and the next child should mix steps from several successful paths — this is the GA answer to EJ's principle, and it is sharper than "(1+1)-ES".
- Building the model of successes (EDA). [Estimation of distribution algorithm — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estimation_of_distribution_algorithm): EDAs "guide the search for the optimum by building and sampling explicit probabilistic models of promising candidate solutions"; the models "reveal a lot of information about the problem being solved… to bias future runs of EDAs on a similar problem"; linkage-learning finds dependencies between variables. Label: `verified` as printed; the cited books (Larrañaga & Lozano 2002; Pelikan et al.) were not read. What it adds: a recorded path is a sample; several paths of a category can be summarised into a model (which steps co-occur, which measures move together) and re-sampled, instead of taking one parent.
- The mistake inside a success has a name in population genetics: hitchhiking. [Genetic hitchhiking — Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genetic_hitchhiking): "an allele changes frequency not because it itself is under natural selection, but because it is near another gene that is undergoing a selective sweep"; "Neutral or even slightly deleterious alleles that happen to be close by on the chromosome 'hitchhike' along with the sweep"; "Recombination can interrupt the process of genetic hitchhiking." The page cites Maynard Smith & Haigh (1974), Fay & Wu (2000) and Good & Desai (2014), "Deleterious Passengers in Adapting Populations." Label: `verified` as printed on Wikipedia; the three papers were not read. What it adds: copying a successful path whole reproduces its passengers; the harness needs recombination (mixing) or an explicit passenger record to drop them.
- What I did not add / could not verify: I did not search for EJ's exact phrase; the researcher (given) reports no source uses it. The building-block hypothesis is contested. The primary papers behind the three Wikipedia pages above were not read, so their empirical claims stay `reported`; only "the page says this" is `verified`.

