2. Who marks a step as a workaround — the agent that ran the task (the writer of R12.7), or the project owner? The answer changes who fills it, and so which artifact it is.
3. Does EJ want the passenger/defect record inside R12.7 (the success path), or as a separate defect line beside R12.2's failure log? It is not a failure, so R12.2's own wording does not hold it.
4. May a design take steps from two recorded paths, or does EJ want to keep "one parent" as the unit and only add the passenger mark? The first is a larger change to R12.7.

Also the threshold for D is EJ's to set; I have only a reported direction.

## Costs and risks

- Step-level measures cost time and tokens (my #8, 「兵聞拙速，未睹巧之久也」 `quoted` — value victory, not duration). The harness is not a campaign, but the rule is that measurement is itself a cost; a path with a check on every step may cost more than the task.
- A workaround mark can become a confession. If the mark is punished, agents will hide it — the deception my #7 names as the way of war (「兵者，詭道也」 `quoted`) works against the record-keeper too. The price of the mark is a trust problem.
- Recombining steps from two paths can break the whole. My #3: 「全國為上，破國次之」 `quoted` — whole is above broken; a path is a whole, and mixing its parts may produce a step set that does not cohere. The check is what keeps it whole; without it, recombination is 攻城, the lowest target.
- Keeping total failures in the record can flood it with flat-landscape entries that read as no-reckoning (my #1). The price is log growth without signal; the contrast field is what distinguishes them.
- A single elite parent under-explores; my #10 (no fixed form) and the researcher's F11/F27/F28–F31 give the risk of converging on a path that is deceptive. More parents cost more storage and more design work.
- Letting the child refuse a step (my #13, 「君命有所不受」 `quoted`) risks re-deriving what the parent already measured; the price is duplicated work.

## How we would know I was right

Three observables, none a gate:
1. In a run folder, the failed record of a check that also passed names the differing step (or says none differed); a failed record of a check that has never passed says so and invents no cause. Reading the two records shows the field is doing work.
2. Plant the brief's own case — a fake engine that returns nothing and a run that calls the real binary, on a task that then passes. The recorded path shows the substitution as a workaround and the passenger line. If the next similar run avoids that substitution, the mark did work; if it repeats it, the mark did not.
3. Take two recorded successful parents of one category, build a child that takes a trusted step from each, and run it. Report the child's value on the pre-named measure against each parent. The claim "recombining parts beats copying one parent" survives if the child does better; it fails if not. Report the result, never block on it.

